David Damerell <damerell@chiark.greenend.org.uk> writes:

> >  So if Nethack's RNG was a poor one, it wouldn't produce 
> >patterns?
> 
> It might or might not produce patterns. In practice the human brain is
> very good at pattern matching and it would be pretty hard to produce an
> RNG that did not produce something the brain would recognise.

Strongly depends how the data is presented graphically 
(i.e., whether the representation stresses the 'patterns').  
Simplest example:  Take a serious linear congruence generator, 
look at a 2D-plot with coordinates (x_i, x_{i+1}) -- very few 
patterns to be seen, if any.  Now take a look at 3D-representation 
with coordinates (x_i, x_{i+1}, x_{i+2}).  Quite some 
pattern, right?  ;-)  For patterns in data, the old proverb 
about absence of evidence ist particular true.  

